import heapq
from collections.abc import Iterable, Iterator

def merge_sorted(*sources: Iterable[int]) -> Iterator[int]:
    iterators = [iter(source) for source in sources]
    heap: list[tuple[int, int]] = []
    for index, iterator in enumerate(iterators):
        try:
            value = next(iterator)
        except StopIteration:
            continue
        heap.append((value, index))
    heapq.heapify(heap)
    while heap:
        value, index = heapq.heappop(heap)
        yield value
        try:
            following = next(iterators[index])
        except StopIteration:
            continue
        heapq.heappush(heap, (following, index))

Each source must already be sorted in ascending order. Empty sources are ignored, and passing no sources produces an empty iterator. The function retains duplicates. The source index breaks ties without comparing iterator objects. It stores at most one pending value per nonempty source, so it does not materialize all the input. If there are k sources and n output elements, heap operations take O(n log k) time and O(k) auxiliary space. An infinite source can be used when its values allow the caller's requested output prefix to be produced.

use std::num::ParseIntError;

fn parse_numbers(input: &str) -> Result<Vec<u32>, ParseIntError> {
    if input.trim().is_empty() {
        return Ok(Vec::new());
    }
    input.split(',').map(|part| part.trim().parse()).collect()
}

fn main() {
    assert_eq!(parse_numbers("10, 20,30").unwrap(), vec![10, 20, 30]);
    assert_eq!(parse_numbers("   ").unwrap(), Vec::<u32>::new());
    assert!(parse_numbers("1,,3").is_err());
    assert!(parse_numbers("-1").is_err());
    assert!(parse_numbers("4294967296").is_err());
}

The iterator yields a Result for every field. Collecting into Result<Vec<u32>, ParseIntError> stops at the first error and returns it to the caller. A completely empty input is handled explicitly. An empty field inside a nonempty list remains an error, so a missing value cannot disappear unnoticed. Trimming whitespace does not make negative numbers or values above the u32 maximum valid. Applications that need to identify the exact bad field can attach the field index to their error type. That is a separate interface choice; the function above preserves the standard parsing error.
